  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "Philosophers adjudicate between the two principles by eliciting and weighing intuitions about hypothetical cases.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "The principle of alternate possibilities states that a person is morally responsible for what he has done only if he could have done otherwise.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "Sourcehood theorists maintain that moral responsibility requires only that the action issue from the agent in the appropriate way.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Leeway theorists maintain that moral responsibility requires access to alternative possibilities.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "John Martin Fischer's semi-compatibilism holds that moral responsibility is compatible with determinism even if the freedom to do otherwise is not.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "Harry Frankfurt published \"Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility\" in 1969.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "Frankfurt published \"Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility\" in 1962.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "Black functions in the case as a counterfactual intervener who would force Jones's decision had Jones shown signs of deciding otherwise.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "Black never intervenes in the course of events as Frankfurt describes them.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "Jones reaches his decision on the basis of his own deliberation and reasons.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "The counterfactual intervener plays no causal role in the agent's actual decision in a Frankfurt-style case.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "The agent in a Frankfurt-style case lacks the power to decide otherwise.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "The flicker of freedom strategy claims that the agent in a Frankfurt case retains a residual alternative, such as beginning to decide otherwise.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "The flicker of freedom strategy was devised to defend Frankfurt's rejection of the principle of alternate possibilities.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
