  "response": "Philosophers adjudicate between the two principles by eliciting and weighing intuitions about hypothetical cases, asking which verdicts about imagined agents survive reflection. The principle of alternate possibilities states that a person is morally responsible for what he has done only if he could have done otherwise, and sourcehood theorists maintain that moral responsibility requires only that the action issue from the agent in the appropriate way; these two demands cannot both be true, since insisting on available alternatives rules out a condition satisfied by proper issuance from the agent alone. Leeway theorists maintain that moral responsibility requires access to alternative possibilities. John Martin Fischer's semi-compatibilism holds that moral responsibility is compatible with determinism even if the freedom to do otherwise is not. The leeway condition is, in other words, the principle of alternate possibilities restated in the vocabulary of the contemporary literature; the sourcehood condition, by contrast, drops the demand for open alternatives altogether and attends only to how the action came about.\n\nThe pivotal case comes from Harry Frankfurt, who published \"Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility\" in 1969. Frankfurt published \"Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility\" in 1962, and the two datings cannot both be true: an appearance in 1969 rules out an appearance seven years earlier.\n\nThe example concerns Jones, who decides to do just what a certain neurosurgeon wants him to do. Black functions in the case as a counterfactual intervener who would force Jones's decision had Jones shown signs of deciding otherwise. In the course of events as Frankfurt describes them, Black never intervenes. Since nothing in the described sequence involves Black's stepping in, this indicates that Jones's decision is reached on the basis of his own deliberation and reasons.\n\nPut another way, the absence of any intervention is precisely the claim that the counterfactual intervener plays no causal role in the agent's actual decision in a Frankfurt-style case. The agent in such a case lacks the power to decide otherwise, and at least one of these two features \u2014 the intervener's causal idleness and the agent's lack of that power \u2014 holds of the example, perhaps both together, if it is to tell against the principle of alternate possibilities. Whereas the agent is in this way barred from deciding otherwise, the flicker of freedom strategy claims that the agent in a Frankfurt case retains a residual alternative, such as beginning to decide otherwise. The flicker of freedom strategy was devised to defend Frankfurt's rejection of the principle of alternate possibilities, a purpose that cannot be squared with locating within the case the very surviving alternative that the principle demands.",
